  "summary": "Marin County Sheriff Jamie Scardina is asking the Board of Supervisors to approve installing 31 Flock-operated ALPR cameras at nine choke-point locations in unincorporated areas to deter theft and aid investigations. The proposal follows a 2022 settlement with the ACLU/EFF over the sheriff's prior Vigilant Solutions system sharing data with ICE and out-of-state agencies, and includes a 30-day retention policy and in-state-only sharing.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Our objective here is to make the streets of Marin County safer by deterring any criminal activity that comes through our county",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "The group claimed the sheriff's office was violating state law by sharing license plate data with U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement and other federal and out-of-state law enforcement agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "All data would be permanently deleted after 30 days on a rolling basis.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock has a much greater potential to violate civil rights and create a dangerous level of surveillance, particularly in communities like Marin City where people of color are overpoliced already.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    }
  ],
